<?nvs
// Every field of one request, read back by the program answering it.
//
// This leg runs under `nvs run --request examples/request-fields.nvsr`, so the
// request below is a real one: `examples/request-fields.nvsr` describes it in
// the format `crates/nvs-test/src/request.rs` owns, and every line printed here
// is a question this program asked of the carrier. Without the `--request`
// argument the first line is `Core\Request::method()` refusing, which is
// `rule:security/request-state-throws-in-an-isolate` and not this file's fact.
//
// Nothing here is a superglobal: `$_GET`, `$_POST`, `$_COOKIE` and `$_REQUEST`
// have no replacement of any kind, and every reading of a request is a member
// (`rule:statements/no-host-populated-variables`). Every value that came from
// outside the process arrives `tainted`, which is why `echo` is the safe thing
// to do with one and a sink is not.

// The verb, as a case of `Core\Http\Method` rather than a string a handler
// would have to spell two ways. A `HEAD` request answers `Get` here, with
// `isHead()` carrying the difference, so a `Get`-only route table still matches.
if (Core\Request::method() == Core\Http\Method::Post) {
    echo "method is Post\n";
}

// The path with the matched mount's prefix already removed, so an application
// reads the same paths wherever it is mounted; the query is read by name and
// never decoded twice.
echo 'path is ', Core\Request::path(), "\n";
echo 'query id is ', Core\Request::query('id') ?? '', "\n";

// A field is matched without regard to case, as RFC 9110 § 5.1 compares one,
// and a cookie is one pair out of the single `Cookie` line a peer sent.
echo 'header accept is ', Core\Request::header('accept') ?? 'none', "\n";
echo 'cookie session is ', Core\Request::cookie('session') ?? 'none', "\n";

// The submitted form, read out of the body's octets under the same bracket
// convention `query()` uses.
echo 'field name is ', Core\Request::post('name') ?? '', "\n";

// The three facts the request arrived *on* rather than in. None of them is
// derived here: `rule:http-server/trusted-proxies-is-empty-and-empty-reads-nothing`'s
// walk settles the address and the scheme at the door, and a request that
// crossed a file states what that walk concluded. A forwarded header from a
// peer nothing trusts changes none of these — it is readable through
// `header()`, and believed by nothing.
//
// `clientIp()` is nullable because a request can genuinely arrive with no
// address to report; `scheme()` is not, because every request arrived over one.
// `host()` is the `Host` line folded the way the server compares a host mount,
// which is what a host-mounted deployment looks its tenant up by.
echo 'client ip is ', Core\Request::clientIp() ?? 'none', "\n";
echo 'scheme is ', Core\Request::scheme(), "\n";
echo 'host is ', Core\Request::host() ?? 'none', "\n";
